mailove (3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Hostile CSS in a message can no longer reach the network the way it could.
    Enabling remote content for a message now loads images and nothing else:
    remote stylesheets (<link>, @import) and web fonts stay blocked, closing
    the channel that lets a sender rewrite a message's CSS after it was
    delivered and read character widths back off the screen. No real mail
    uses either - the major clients strip them - so nothing legitimate is
    lost.
  * Link sanitizing now reads a URL the way the browser does. Character
    references and stray tabs or newlines inside a scheme
    ("&#106;avascript:", "java&Tab;script:") used to slip past the check that
    reads the raw text, and a '>' inside a quoted attribute cut a tag short so
    the rest of it went unexamined. Schemes are now allow-listed rather than
    blocked by name, and mailove: is refused in sender markup.
  * Mailove tells you when a newer version exists: one HEAD request to the
    project's release page, 30 seconds after launch and when Settings is
    opened, at most once a day. It sends nothing but the version already in
    the User-Agent, the answer is remembered so the marker shows at once on
    the next start, and the whole thing is off with one switch in Advanced
    settings (update/checkEnabled).
  * Settings backup fixes. The spare copy was written from the settings file
    as it was read at *startup*, so it lagged a session behind what you had
    actually configured; it now follows the file and is taken as the client
    shuts down. An empty settings file - which is what a truncated write
    leaves behind - is no longer copied over a good spare. And the backup is
    never read back: Mailove will not restore from it on its own, so the
    spare can no longer write itself over a live config. Putting it back is a
    deliberate act with a file manager.
  * Fixed labels, shortcuts and layout silently reverting. Window and UI state
    used QML's Settings type, which holds every key in memory from startup and
    rewrites all of them on any one change - so anything a second Mailove or a
    text editor had altered meanwhile was quietly put back to whatever that
    run started with. Each key is now written on its own, read-modify-written
    against the file as it stands, and a key this build does not know is left
    untouched.

 -- Daniel Duris <dusoft@staznosti.sk>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 19:00:00 +0200

mailove (3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Activity log in Settings (Ctrl+Shift+L): the last 5000 lines Mailove
    logged, copyable into a bug report with addresses masked, mirrored to a
    self-recycling file that never needs clearing. Backed up settings are
    saved on change at exit, so a bad write no longer loses your labels,
    shortcuts and layout.
  * Unread badges finally tell the truth. The server is asked which mail is
    actually unread - per folder, on the open account and in the background -
    so a folder read elsewhere loses its pill without being clicked, and a
    subfolder gains one before you open it.
  * IMAP correctness: writes and reads now select their folder atomically,
    fixing a spam-delete that ran against the inbox and searches that read
    the wrong mailbox; the folder-sync pass no longer loops forever; push
    reconnects cleanly instead of churning.
  * Reclaim disk space no longer freezes the app - it compacts a copy and
    swaps it in, off the GUI thread. Startup migrations stop re-running once
    done.
  * Fixes: message-list selection survives arrow/page keys and extends with
    Shift; log lines collapse repeats and cap length; several QML errors
    (TabBar, folder click, tooltips) silenced; tooltips instant and only
    where they add something.
  * Problems are written down now. Settings keeps the last 5000 lines
    Mailove logged (Ctrl+Shift+L) and hands them to the clipboard or a file
    for a bug report, with email addresses masked on the way out. The same
    lines are mirrored to one file under ~/.local/state/mailove, which
    recycles in place at 5000 lines: no rotation, no second copy, and
    nothing to remember to delete.
  * Nothing waits for the network any more. Deleting, moving, filing spam,
    renaming a folder and marking mail read all happen the moment you ask,
    online or off, and are pushed to the server afterwards. The five
    commands that used to answer "Not connected" and do nothing now simply
    work on a train.
  * Changes are made as transactions rather than hoped for. Nothing is
    destroyed locally until the server agrees it is gone, so a change the
    server refuses is undone: the message reappears, the folder reverts to
    its old name, the deleted folder comes back with its mail. A
    breadcrumb says so at the time, and Settings keeps a "Failed changes"
    list naming the mail, the reason and when - each row with Retry and
    Discard - so "why did that come back" still has an answer an hour
    later.
  * A message read on another device no longer keeps its unread badge here
    for good - the "12 new messages, nothing new inside" inbox. Opening a
    folder now asks the server about the messages it still counts as
    unread, and corrects the ones that were read elsewhere. It asks about
    the unread mail rather than the folder, so the cost is the size of the
    badge, not the size of the mailbox.
  * A folder you move mail into says so while the move is still queued -
    "3 messages moved here - will appear when you reconnect" - so mail
    filed offline is accounted for rather than seeming to vanish between
    the folder it left and the one it has not reached yet.
  * A folder is never synced while it still owes the server a change,
    which closes the window where a refresh could read the old state back
    and quietly undo what you had just done.
  * DKIM and OpenPGP verify the message that travelled, not KMime's
    re-assembly of it. KMime inserts a blank line where a nested MIME part
    closes tight against its parent boundary (KDE bug 523826) - the shape
    Gmail produces on replies with attachments - so the body no longer
    hashed to what the sender signed and good mail was reported as
    "modified after signing". Bodies now arrive as two octet-exact halves
    over the existing IMAP session (headers and text are fetched
    separately, which sidesteps the re-assembly entirely) and are parsed
    frozen, so the bytes that get hashed are the bytes off the wire. The
    same freeze covers JMAP blobs and whole cached bodies.
  * On first start after the upgrade, cached bodies from the last month are
    re-fetched once and their authentication verdicts and spam scores
    recomputed against the faithful bytes. Older mail keeps its stored
    verdict; imported archives and your own spam/not-spam answers are
    never touched.
  * "Modified after signing" is now trustworthy: when the badge appears on
    freshly fetched mail, the body really does differ from what was signed
    - typically a forwarder or list re-encoding it, which the ARC seals
    beside it will corroborate.
  * When a message carries several DKIM signatures - the sender's plus a
    forwarder's - the verdict shown is the most informative one, not
    whichever signature happened to be listed last: a valid signature
    outranks a body mismatch, a mismatch outranks a failure, a failure
    outranks a key that cannot be fetched. Ties go to the sender's own
    signature.
  * A body fetch that yields only half a message is dropped and re-fetched
    instead of being shown (or cached, or spam-scored) as a headerless
    message; the cache refuses headerless bodies outright and heals any it
    finds.
  * fidelitytool now measures the exact fetch path the app uses and prints
    wire-vs-fetched octet comparisons per message; rawfidelitytest asserts
    the frozen round-trip offline and reports when a fixed KMime makes the
    workaround unnecessary.
  * One-time cache upgrades no longer hold up the launch. They used to run
    before the window appeared, which on a large cache meant minutes of
    apparently nothing happening; they now run afterwards in a dialog that
    names the step, counts it off, and says roughly how much longer it
    needs. Background syncing pauses while they run and resumes by itself,
    and a run interrupted by a quit picks up where it left off.
  * Mark read/unread has a shortcut of its own (M), and spam is now one key
    in both directions (J) instead of two. Both follow the message they act
    on: the key marks read when it is unread and unread when it is read,
    spam when it is not and not spam when it is. The right-click menu shows
    each command's key beside it.
  * Sending survives the connection now. A message written offline is kept
    as the exact bytes that will go on the wire and sent when you reconnect,
    so quitting, crashing or a dead connection can no longer lose a pressed
    Send - and a send interrupted mid-flight says "may already have been
    sent" rather than quietly going out twice. Online, sending is unchanged:
    it goes straight out, with no queue and no Outbox appearing for a
    moment. The Outbox shows up in the folder list only while something is
    waiting in it, offering Cancel, Retry now, and Edit for the messages the
    composer can rebuild (not encrypted mail or attachments). Retrying knows
    the difference between "try later" and "no": a dead connection or a busy
    server backs off and tries again, an address the server rejected fails
    once and waits for you.
  * Undo send, off by default (Settings - General). Turned on, each message
    is held for a few seconds before it goes: an "Undo send" button counts
    the hold down beside Compose, Ctrl+Z does the same from anywhere, and
    the message comes back into the composer as if Send had never been
    pressed. The delay is adjustable in Advanced, and the key is rebindable
    like every other one.
  * Wording: counts now read "1 moved to trash" rather than "1 message(s)
    moved to trash", and Settings no longer claims the Advanced page saves
    itself - that page has always had its own Apply button.

 -- Daniel Duris <dusoft@staznosti.sk>  Mon, 18 Aug 2026 17:30:00 +0200

mailove (2.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A new spam rule: mail from a top-level domain your own sent mail never
    goes to scores slightly higher. The profile is built from the recipients
    of your own Sent mail, per account — someone writing to .sk and .com is
    not expecting first contact from .top — and it only ever corroborates:
    on its own it cannot mark anything, and a domain you already have a
    history with is never judged by the country it is in. Silent until
    enough sent mail exists to describe a habit (spam/tldMinSample).
  * Every spam rule's weight is now a setting, [spamrules] in advanced.conf:
    one key per rule id as printed in the "Why?" tooltip, so a rule that
    misfires on your mail can be found from the message it misfired on and
    turned down, or off with 0 — which removes it from the tooltip too.
    Changes apply to mail scored from now on; stored verdicts keep the score
    and the explanation they were given.
  * The advanced-settings groups are ordered alphabetically and each carries
    a heading and a plain-language explainer — in the reference beside the
    editor and as comments in the seeded file alike.
  * The spam threshold can no longer be set to 0, which would have marked
    every message in the mailbox: a message no rule fires on scores exactly 0.
  * Sender pictures are fetched at twice the size they are shown at, so they
    stay sharp on HiDPI screens. A few KB more, once per address per year.
  * New spam rules for mail sent through hacked websites, adapted from
    kawaiipantsu/spamassassin-rules: PHP code existing only in memory
    (php-eval-source), scripts mailing out of a CMS tree —
    WordPress or Joomla (php-cms-origin), a years-unmaintained PHPMailer
    (vulnerable-mailer), and .php links fed random tokens (hacked-php-url).
    An ordinary shop's PHP-sent receipt stays well below the threshold.
  * The link rules (link-text-mismatch, url-*) now answer to [spamrules]
    like every other rule; previously their weights were fixed.
  * Microsoft's compauth verdict (composite authentication, stamped by
    Microsoft 365) now counts: compauth=fail scores like any other
    authentication failure — including revoking the known-correspondent
    exemption for the spoofs of DMARC-less domains it exists to catch — and
    compauth=pass like a pass. The account's authentication checkbox
    remains the master switch; per-method spam/trustSpf, trustDkim,
    trustDmarc, trustArc and trustCompauth keys in advanced.conf drop one
    method's verdicts without giving up the rest (SPF broken by forwarding,
    a noisy Microsoft tenant). A distrusted method disappears from the
    message's authentication badge too, so what is shown is always what
    was counted; compauth appears there alongside the others.
  * The server-verdict rules read more providers' headers: Barracuda
    (X-Barracuda-Spam-Status), Proofpoint incl. iCloud
    (X-Proofpoint-Spam-Details), Yandex, Google Workspace (X-Gm-Spam,
    X-Gm-Phishy) and GMX/Web.de (X-UI-Filterresults). All feed the existing
    upstream rules and their [spamrules] weights; the forgery check (header
    must be added in transit) applies unchanged.
  * spf=softfail is scored apart from an outright failure: its own
    lower-weighted rule (auth-softfail), never revoking the known-sender
    exemption — forwarded mail is what softfail looks like — and shown in
    orange rather than red in the authentication badge.
  * The message viewer's authentication badges speak one language. The
    server's verdicts read as glyph-first pairs — SPF / DKIM / DMARC / ARC /
    COMPAUTH with green, orange or red check marks, a softfail orange rather
    than red, and several signatures for one method kept as "✓, ✗" so the
    comma still says there were two. Mailove's own DKIM and ARC verification
    stands at full size above them, likewise glyph-first.
  * Those badges explain themselves on hover: each tooltip is one block per
    method — the acronym in bold, a plain question ("SPF: Is the sending
    server allowed to send for this domain?"), the answer as a bold word
    (Yes / No / Warning / Not checked), and directly beneath it that
    method's own slice of the raw header as evidence.
  * The server's DKIM verdict is no longer shown beside mailove's own: two
    DKIM answers side by side read as a contradiction when they are really
    one check knowing more than the other (alignment, not just validity).
    The server's DKIM evidence moved into the tooltip of the badge that
    stays.
  * Authentication wording, tidied: every badge names the check it speaks
    for ("Modified after DKIM signing", "Modified after OpenPGP signing"),
    a signature from the wrong domain names both domains rather than saying
    only that they differ, and every string in the row starts with a
    capital.
  * The badge row no longer shifts as messages change: its labels share a
    baseline, so a taller glyph in one of them cannot nudge its neighbours
    up or down.
  * Fixed: the ARC exemption never actually applied — arc=pass was not
    extracted from Authentication-Results, so mailing-list mail whose
    SPF/DKIM broke in transit could be wrongly marked. The parsing now
    lives in spamheuristics.cpp under test.
  * More hacked-website coverage: links into WordPress code directories
    (hacked-wordpress-link, from martinschaible/rspamd-rules), scripts
    mailing from /.well-known/ or the PHPUnit RCE path, and a URL-shortener
    list grown from 16 to 116 services. Newsletter media links under
    wp-content/uploads are deliberately not matched.

 -- Daniel Duris <dusoft@staznosti.sk>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

mailove (2.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Advanced settings, under Settings -> Advanced: everything with no widget of
    its own — sync pacing, connection counts, protocol timeouts, spam weights,
    cache thresholds, database timeouts — edited as plain INI in
    ~/.config/mailove/advanced.conf, with a searchable reference beside the
    editor generated from the same schema the values are read through.
  * A value outside its range is corrected on save and an unknown key ignored
    with a warning; neither stops the rest of the file from applying. Only a
    syntax error is refused, and it names the line it is on. Comments, blank
    lines and ordering survive exactly as typed.
  * Keys that only take effect on restart say so, under the Apply button, and
    keep saying so after the save rather than the moment before it.
  * Emptying the file removes it and restores stock behaviour; blank lines at
    the top and bottom are trimmed, the ones between sections left alone.
  * No secret is ever written to that file. An OAuth client secret typed there
    is moved into the system wallet on save and the line rewritten to @wallet
    — including for a file edited by hand outside the client, which is swept
    at startup. Nothing in the client can read a secret back out of the file.
  * Sender pictures (Gravatar), off by default: asking gravatar.com for a
    picture tells gravatar.com that the address was seen, so nothing is
    requested or cached until avatars/enabled says so. The address never
    leaves the machine — what goes out is its SHA-256 — and answers are kept
    for a year, misses for a month.
  * How long a message must stay open before it counts as read is now a number
    of seconds rather than on or off, decimals included. 0 leaves it unread
    until marked read by hand, and moving through a list no longer marks
    everything on the way past.
  * The message context menu alternates instead of offering the command that
    does nothing: Mark read or Mark unread by the message's own state, Mark as
    spam or Not spam by its mark — and always Not spam in the junk folder,
    where a message put there by a server-side rule carries no local score.
  * DKIM key records are cached for at least half an hour rather than five
    minutes. Key rotation publishes a new selector, so a stale record for the
    old one is never asked for again.

